Vite does not support a callback for `resolve.conditions` — the maintainer explicitly declined it: "We don't want to accept a function for now as it's difficult to merge if other plugins were to add more conditions" [4]. Instead, import Vite's default list and spread it yourself: use `defaultClientConditions` for client builds or `defaultServerConditions` for server builds [4]. `defaultClientConditions` is documented at https://vite.dev/config/shared-options#resolve-conditions [4].

```js
import { defaultClientConditions } from 'vite'

export default defineConfig({
  resolve: {
    conditions: ['workspace', ...defaultClientConditions],
  },
})
```

The reporter confirmed this approach "solves the issue cleanly" and closed the issue [5].

The failure is an upstream Rolldown issue: it's believed to be caused by rolldown/rolldown#9964, related to the `lazyBarrel` feature [2]. You can work around it by disabling that feature in your config [2]:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  build: {
    rolldownOptions: {
      experimental: {
        lazyBarrel: false
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Multiple users confirmed this workaround resolves the problem [3][4]. Alternatively, upgrading to Rolldown v1.1.4 should work, since rolldown/rolldown#10071 temporarily disabled the `lazyBarrel` feature [1].

Vite 8.0.13 has a regression where const enums used as types in casting disappear from the bundled output, whereas in 8.0.12 they were still present as regular compiled enums [2]. The issue is that a direct `as` cast causes the enum to be dropped from the outputted code [4]. This is a regression labeled as an upstream bug [1][2].

It's fixed in 8.0.14 [1]. As a workaround, avoid the direct `as` part, e.g.:

```ts
class Foo {
  // casting causes Phase to not be in the outputted code
  #foo = ([Phase.idle] as [Phase])[0];

  test() {
    return this.#foo;
  }
}
```
[4]

The failure is a Rolldown CJS namespace interop issue, not something specific to `backbone-relational`: a CJS namespace import snapshots the current `module.exports` properties before a later CJS side-effect import mutates the same export object [2]. That's why `import * as Backbone from 'backbone'` followed by `import 'backbone-relational'` works in Vite dev but fails in the Vite 8 build, while default and named imports still work because they keep/read the original CJS export object [2]. The maintainers closed it as wont-fix since the new behavior is more aligned with ESM semantics [4]. As a workaround, use a default import instead of a namespace import [1]:

```js
import Backbone from "backbone";
import "backbone-relational";

Backbone.RelationalModel.extend({});
```

This is a reported bug in Vite 8: in `build()` library mode, `resolve.alias` is ignored unless you also move or copy it under `build.rolldownOptions` [4]. The reporter notes the error is not reproduced at Vite 7 [1]. The excerpts don't include a root-cause explanation, so I can't say why it happens internally—only that it's tracked as issue #22410 [4].

Workaround from the report [4]:

```js
await build({
  configFile: false,
  resolve: { alias: { "@": path.resolve("./src") } },
  build: {
    rolldownOptions: {
      resolve: { alias: { "@": path.resolve("./src") } },
    },
    // ...
  }
})
```

There's no built-in option yet — the `maxWarnings` config option is only a suggested solution in the open issue [1]. The maintainers currently prefer a plugin approach [3]. You can use a plugin that counts warnings via `onLog` and throws in `buildEnd` [4]:

```ts
function failOnWarningsPlugin(): Rolldown.Plugin {
  let warningCount = 0;
  return {
    name: 'fail-on-warnings',
    onLog(level) {
      if (level === 'warn') warningCount++;
    },
    buildEnd() {
      if (!warningCount) return;
      throw new Error(`${warningCount} warnings present`);
    },
  };
}
```

Note that throwing will also log an error stack trace, and `process.exit` is discouraged due to Node.js stdio flushing issues [5].

This is expected behavior, not a Vite bug. Unlike webpack, Vite treats `.jsx` and `.js` similarly, and because your `package.json` has `"type": "module"`, the CommonJS default export isn't unwrapped automatically — so you need to access `.default` on the import [3]. This relates to Vite's consistent CommonJS interop change [4].

```js
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg';
// use FroalaEditorComponent.default
```

The failure is a compatibility issue with esbuild versions 0.27.7 and later: after upgrading Vite to `7.3.2`, `@vitejs/plugin-legacy` starts failing during the legacy downgrade/transformation flow, and forcing `esbuild` to `0.27.4` makes the problem disappear [5]. It reproduces even with a minimal legacy config such as `targets: ['defaults', 'not IE 11']` [3]. A Vite member identified the root cause as the upstream esbuild issue https://github.com/evanw/esbuild/issues/4436 [4], and the tracking approach is to wait for that upstream fix rather than reverting esbuild [2]. The workaround is an override:

```json
{
  "overrides": {
    "esbuild": "0.27.4"
  }
}
```

Vite 8's dependency optimization reorders ECharts internals: in the optimized output, the `install` method runs before `init_install` has executed, causing `Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'type')`, whereas Vite 5.4.11 ran `init_install` first [5]. The issue is labeled "bug: upstream" and reproduces on `vite@8.0.3` (also with `rolldown@1.0.0-rc.13`), while `vite@5.4.11` behaves normally [1][3]. A member notes it "seems this works with Vite 8.0.14+" [1]. As a dev workaround, set `optimizeDeps.rolldownOptions.output.strictExecutionOrder: true` [4]:

```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  optimizeDeps: {
    rolldownOptions: {
      output: {
        strictExecutionOrder: true
      }
    }
  },
});
```

The excerpts don't explain the underlying cause beyond it being an upstream issue [1].

The `resolve.tsconfigPaths` option in `vite.config.ts` was reported as not working in Vite 8 [1]. A fix landed: one reporter confirmed that in dev mode it now works normally after the fix [2]. Note that in build mode it previously appeared to always resolve tsconfig paths regardless of the option, but a maintainer clarified that was a resolver bug, not intended behavior [4][5]. So after the fix, set the option in your config as usual:

```ts
export default defineConfig({
  resolve: {
    tsconfigPaths: true,
  },
})
```

The excerpts don't specify which Vite 8 patch version contains the fix, so check that you're on a release that includes it.

In Vite 8, `import.meta.url` gets compiled to `{}.url` (so `fileURLToPath` receives `undefined`) because the `platform` option defaults to `'browser'` rather than `'node'` for `cjs` output [1][4]. A maintainer states this is expected behavior: Vite's lib mode is intended for browser-oriented libraries, and the `'node'`-for-`cjs` default you saw is Rolldown's default, not Vite's — `platform` didn't exist in Rollup, so it didn't exist in Vite v7 [2][4]. The fix is to set `build.rolldownOptions.platform` explicitly [2]:

```js
build: {
  rolldownOptions: {
    platform: 'node'
  }
}
```

With `platform: "node"`, the output becomes `require("url").pathToFileURL(__filename).href` instead of `{}.url` [1].

The TypeScript error (2769) happens because of a TypeScript limitation, not a Vite bug: when the config is returned from an async function, the `minify` string literal widens and no longer matches the expected type [2][3]. The fix is to add `as const` [2], e.g.:

```ts
export default defineConfig(async () => ({
  plugins: [react()],
  build: {
    minify: 'terser' as const,
  },
}))
```

The bug is in the upstream bundler, not in your code: Rollup was dropping the `[Symbol.dispose]` member when compiling `using` declarations, so the object came out empty [1][3]. It's fixed by Rollup 4.53.4 [3], so upgrading Rollup (and thus Vite's bundled Rollup) resolves it.

Until then, these patterns survive `vite build` [2]:

```typescript
// Factory function
function disposable(fn: () => void): Disposable {
  return { [Symbol.dispose]: fn }
}
using _ = disposable(() => console.log('disposed'))

// Class
class MyDisposable implements Disposable {
  [Symbol.dispose]() { console.log('disposed') }
}
using _ = new MyDisposable()
```

Note that the class form is listed among the workarounds that work [2], so if your class still compiles to an empty object, the excerpts don't cover that case — the fix is the Rollup 4.53.4 upgrade [3].

Vite 7.2.x removes `/*! ... */` comments because esbuild treats them as legal comments and strips them during minification [1][4]. As bluwy explains, `/*! */` is treated as a legal comment by most tools and isn't a reliable way to mark comments as "important," so relying on that behavior isn't robust if legal comments are meant to be handled externally [1].

The workaround is to set `esbuild.legalComments: 'inline'` [2][3]:

```js
export default {
  esbuild: {
    legalComments: 'inline',
  },
}
```

Yes — starting in 7.1.8, vite-rolldown removes comments even when `minify: false` is set, so `/* @vite-ignore */` and `/* webpackIgnore: true */` comments are stripped from the output; in 7.1.7 they were preserved [2]. The cause is single-use var inlining for DCE in Rolldown, and it's tracked as a regression [2][5]. As a workaround, put the comment before the string rather than before the variable reference [3]:

```js
export async function init() {
  const bar = /* @vite-ignore */ /* webpackIgnore: true */ 'some-url'.slice(0);
  return await import(bar);
}
```

Vite doesn't treat `*.php` files specially, so it doesn't know about them — that's expected behavior [4]. The regression in 7.1 is likely due to PR #20391 [5], and the maintainers recommend triggering a full reload via a custom plugin instead [1]. You can work around it with:

```js
plugins: [
	{
		name: 'reload-non-vite-code',
		hotUpdate(ctx) {
			if (ctx.file.endsWith(".php")) {
				ctx.server.environments.client.hot.send({
					type: 'full-reload',
				})
			}
		},
	}
]
```

Alternatively, `vite-plugin-full-reload` should work [4].

You can't currently get both behaviors with `server.proxy`'s `auth` option: when the client sends its own `authorization` header, the basic auth header from the `auth` option is not sent to the upstream [3]. This is described as a bug in http-proxy, which is bundled within vite [3].

The reported config is:

```js
const proxy = {
  '/api/service1': {
    target: 'http://localhost:80/endpoint/requiring/basic-auth',
    auth: 'user1:pass1',
  },
};
```

The excerpts don't contain a workaround or a fix for this case.

There's no built-in option for it. The `build.modulePreload` option only disables preloading of JS chunks, not CSS [4], and a maintainer said Vite "can add a option to disable the automatic loading of CSS" but that it doesn't exist yet [5]. The issue was closed as stale, with the use case redirected to issue #13952 [1].

As a workaround, you can remove the import-analysis build plugin. In Vite 8 the plugin name is `native:import-analysis-build` (older guidance suggested `vite:build-import-analysis`) [2]:

```js
{
  name: 'preload-remover',
  configResolved(config) {
    const pI = config.plugins.findIndex(
      (p) => p.name === 'native:import-analysis-build',
    );
    (config.plugins as Plugin[]).splice(pI, 1);
  },
}
```

Note that a single CSS chunk may be imported by both router-managed and non-managed dynamic imports, in which case you'd have to manage all CSS assets yourself [5].

The hang is an upstream Rollup issue: it was fixed in rollup v4.44.0 [1], and the relevant Rollup change only altered the default of `maxParallelFileOps` [4]. As a workaround, set `build.rollupOptions.maxParallelFileOps: 100` [2][3]:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  build: {
    rollupOptions: {
      maxParallelFileOps: 100,
    },
  },
}
```

One reporter saw it hang at CSS transformation in a lib-mode build with more than 100 input and output files [2].

Because Sass's modern API strips the alias before Vite sees the path: Sass passes `@/../node_modules/bootstrap/scss/_functions` to Vite as `node_modules/bootstrap/scss/_functions`, so the alias can't be resolved [4]. This is a limitation of Sass's modern API with no easy fix, so the issue was closed as won't-fix [1][4]. It only happens with the modern API, which is the default from Vite 6 [3]. As a workaround, set the legacy API:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  css: {
    preprocessorOptions: {
      sass: { api: 'legacy' }
    }
  }
}
```

(For SCSS specifically, use `scss.api: 'legacy'` [2].)

You can't turn off the WebSocket: a Vite maintainer states "WebSocket is used for features other than HMR so it cannot be disabled" [4]. To disable HMR, use the existing `server.hmr: false` option [5]:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  server: {
    hmr: false,
  },
}
```

If your goal is to emulate production, use `vite preview` instead of `vite dev`, since `vite dev` isn't meant for that [5]. The only WebSocket workarounds mentioned are unofficial: muting `setupWebSocket` [1] or a Chrome DevTools override that returns early from `setupWebSocket` [2].
